      <div type="clause" n="Prol. 2">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IIEm">II Em</ref> Prol. 2</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="Prol. 2" corresp="#wit-h"><pb n="186" ed="#liebermann"/>Me eleð<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.2"/> swyðe ⁊ us eallu<hi rend="italic">m</hi> ða unrihtlican ⁊ mænigfealdan gefeoht, ðe betwux us sylfu<hi rend="italic">m</hi> syndan; þon<hi rend="italic">ne</hi> cwæde we:</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="Prol. 2" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="186" ed="#liebermann"/>Me egleð swyðe<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.6"/> ⁊ us eallu<hi rend="italic">m</hi> ða unrihtlican ⁊ mænigfealdan<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.7"/> gefeoht, ðe betwux<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.8"/> us sylfu<hi rend="italic">m</hi> syndu<hi rend="italic">n<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.9"/></hi>; ðon<hi rend="italic">ne</hi> cwæde<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.9"/> we:</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Prol. 2" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="187" ed="#liebermann"/>Et<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.5"/> mihi ualde displicent et nobis omnibus multiplices<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.6-2"/> et iniuste pugne, que inter nos ipsos fiunt<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.7-2"/> (fuerint); unde diximus:</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="§1[b]" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="8" ed="#robertson"/>Me egleð swyðe ⁊ us eallum ða unrihtlican ⁊ mænigfealdan gefeoht ðe betwux us sylfum syndun; ðonne cwæde we:</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="187" ed="#liebermann"/>Mich<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.de.1"/> und uns alle verdriessen<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.de.2"/> sehr die ungesetzlichen und mannigfachen Fehden, die zwischen uns sind; nun<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.de.3"/> bestimmten wir:</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">The unlawful and manifold feuds that exist among us greatly displease me and all of us; now we have determined:</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="9" ed="#robertson"/>I myself and all of us are greatly distressed by the manifold illegal deeds of violence which are in our midst<ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#IIEm_Prol-to-2.robnote.n.1"/>. We have therefore decreed:</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#whitelock"><pb n="429" ed="#whitelock"/>The illegal and manifold conflicts which take place among us distress me and all of us greatly. We decreed then:</ab>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">186</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">swiþe <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">swiþe Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.7" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">186</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">men. -hte <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">men. -hte Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.8" n="8" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">186</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">folgt Rasur B</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">followed by an erasure B</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.9" n="9" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">186</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-don <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-don Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">187</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Hn</hi> [<hi rend="italic">besser</hi>]</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in Hn [better]</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.6-2" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">187</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">i. et m. <hi rend="italic">Hn</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">i. et m. Hn</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.7-2" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">187</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">fi. <hi rend="italic">R;</hi> fu. <hi rend="italic">TMHkBrHn</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">fi. R; fu. TMHkBrHn</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.oe.2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">186</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sic</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">127</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Hn 88, 12 zitiert diese Stelle aus besserer Q-Form, als welche uns erhalten ist.—In Norwegen bedauert in Frostathing-Vorrede König Hakon (1217—63) die vielen Totschläge</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Hn 88, 12 quotes this passage from a better form of Q than the one that has come down to us.—In Norway King Hakon (1217—63), in the preface to the Frostathing law, laments the many homicides</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">127</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">eleð</hi> in H Mittelenglisch.—Nicht etwa jede Fehde abzuschaffen erstrebt II Em; er schränkt sie nur ein</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">eleð</hi> in H is Middle English.—It is not, say, the abolition of every feud that II Em aims at; it merely restricts feud</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">127</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Folgende drei Wörter ständen besser als Beginn von 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The following three words would stand better as the beginning of 1</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IIEm_Prol-to-2.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">296</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. the preface of the <hi rend="italic">Frostathing</hi> Laws (<hi rend="italic">Norges Gamle Love</hi>, ed. Keyser and Munch, <hi rend="smallcaps">i</hi>. p. 121), where King Haakon (1217–63) deplores the many homicides throughout Norway.</seg></note>
      </div>
    </body>
